{"name":"Cobre","hex":"#996515","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[],"entries":[{"title":"El Cobre, Cuba","blocks":[{"text":"El Cobre is a Cuban town and consejo popular of the municipality of Santiago de Cuba, capital of the homonym province, with a population of about 7,000. Mainly known for the Basilica in honour of Our Lady of Charity, Virgen de la Caridad del Cobre, the patron saint of Cuba, it was until recently the site of a large copper mine worked by slaves, free coloured people, and for a while by miners from Cornwall."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/El_Cobre_CF9A5919.jpg/330px-El_Cobre_CF9A5919.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Cobre%2C_Cuba","wikidata":"Q12889951"},{"title":"Rio Cobre","blocks":[{"text":"The Rio Cobre is a river in Jamaica. Its source is in the Rose Hall Mountain in the north-east of Saint Catherine Parish, the headwaters being a writhing of unnamed, seasonally dry tributaries. The highest of these rise just above the 1,135 feet (346 m) contour. From here it flows to meet the Caribbean Sea into the Hunts Bay. It is dammed by the Rio Cobre Dam just above Spanish Town."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Jamaica-FlatBridge.jpg/330px-Jamaica-FlatBridge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rio_Cobre","wikidata":"Q2154356"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Monumento_al_cimarr%C3%B3n_El_Cobre_002.jpg/500px-Monumento_al_cimarr%C3%B3n_El_Cobre_002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Monumento_al_cimarr%C3%B3n_El_Cobre_002.jpg/1280px-Monumento_al_cimarr%C3%B3n_El_Cobre_002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wik